Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: One medium throughout - period live-action storybook, an English nursery of the 1920s. The toys are practical velveteen, tin and hide puppets; they stir and speak only when no grown person is watching.

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Frost ferns on the window. A stocking hangs bulging from the mantel. Wedged in the top sits THE RABBIT - a velveteen rabbit, fat and bunchy, his coat spotted brown and white, real thread whiskers, ears lined with pink sateen, boot-button eyes, a sprig of holly between his paws.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

THE BOY, six, fair hair flattened by sleep, in a long nightshirt, digs out nuts and oranges, a toy engine, chocolate almonds, a clockwork mouse. Then he lifts the Rabbit and holds him hard against his chin.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There were other things in the stocking, but the Rabbit was quite the best of all. For at least two hours the Boy loved him.

Below stairs, a doorbell. Voices of aunts and uncles, a great rustling of tissue paper, the snap of string on new parcels. The Boy is called away and runs.

The Rabbit lies where he was dropped on the nursery floor, the holly slipping out of his paws.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then, in the excitement of looking at all the new presents, the Velveteen Rabbit was forgotten.

INT. NURSERY - AFTERNOON

The toy cupboard stands open. A tin steam-roller and a clockwork mouse hold the middle of the floor. A model boat, most of his paint lost, leans against the skirting. TIMOTHY, a jointed wooden lion, sits very upright with his painted head turned away.

The Rabbit is shoved aside by the steam-roller and comes down soft.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The mechanical toys were very superior, and looked down upon every one else; they were full of modern ideas, and pretended they were real.

The model boat swings his little rigging importantly. Timothy tilts his chin, connected with Government.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Between them all the poor little Rabbit was made to feel himself very insignificant and commonplace.

At the back of the cupboard, in shadow, the SKIN HORSE - a great sagging hide horse, brown coat bald in patches so the seams show underneath, tail nearly pulled bare for bead necklaces.

He leans down and nudges the Rabbit gently upright.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And the only person who was kind to him at all was the Skin Horse.

INT. NURSERY - EVENING

Firelight through the nursery fender. The Rabbit and the Skin Horse lie side by side on the hearthrug, the room otherwise still.

THE RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

THE RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

THE RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept.

The fire settles. The bald patches on the Skin Horse's flank show their old seams.

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

THE RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Rabbit wishes at once that he had not said it. The Skin Horse only smiles.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs, and looks down at his own bright, unworn velveteen.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He longed to become Real, to know what it felt like; and yet the idea of growing shabby and losing his eyes and whiskers was rather sad.

The door bangs. NANA sweeps in - grey-haired, starched apron and cap - going about like a great wind, hustling playthings into cupboards, tin ones clattering.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was a person called Nana who ruled the nursery. She called this "tidying up," and the playthings all hated it, especially the tin ones.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

The night-light burns on the mantelpiece. The Boy stands beside his bed in his nightshirt, hunting under the valance for the china dog that always sleeps with him.

Nana, in a hurry, sees the toy cupboard door standing open, and makes a swoop. She drags the Rabbit out by one ear.

NANA
Here - take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She puts him into the Boy's arms.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night, and for many nights after, the Velveteen Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed.

The Boy hugs him very tight, rolls over on him, pushes him so far under the pillow that the Rabbit can scarcely breathe.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He missed those long moonlight hours in the nursery, when all the house was silent, and his talks with the Skin Horse. But very soon he grew to like it.

Nana's footsteps go away down the passage to her supper. At once the bedclothes lift into a long tunnel, and the Boy pushes the Rabbit through it, whispering.

THE BOY
(whispering)
That's a burrow. Real rabbits live in burrows, like this.

The Boy drops off to sleep with his hands clasped close round the Rabbit, who snuggles down under the little warm chin.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And so time went on, and the little Rabbit was very happy - so happy that he never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier, and his tail becoming unsewn, and all the pink rubbed off his nose where the Boy had kissed him.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring. Apple blossom, a flower border, a thicket of raspberry canes behind it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Spring came, and they had long days in the garden, for wherever the Boy went the Rabbit went too.

The Boy trundles the Rabbit round the paths in a wheelbarrow. A picnic on the grass, crumbs on velvet paws. Under the raspberry canes the Boy builds him a fairy hut of sticks and moss, and scoops burrows in the flower bed to dive him into. Earth on the Rabbit's nose.

A call from the house. The Boy is wanted - he is going out to tea. He runs, and the Rabbit stays where he was dropped, out on the lawn.

The light goes. Dew forms on the worn velveteen.

EXT. GARDEN - NIGHT

Long after dusk. A candle comes bobbing down the path - Nana, holding it high, searching the wet grass. She finds the Rabbit and picks him up: soaked through with dew and quite earthy.

She rubs him off with a corner of her apron, grumbling.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Nana comes in with the candle and the damp Rabbit. The Boy sits up in bed and stretches out both hands.

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

Nana sighs and gives him over. The Boy tucks the Rabbit in beside him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last. He was a toy no longer. He was Real. The Boy himself had said it.

Across the room, in the open cupboard, the Skin Horse stands quite still in the dark.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night he was almost too happy to sleep, and so much love stirred in his little sawdust heart that it almost burst.

The night-light flickers on two dull boot-button eyes that have long ago lost their polish.

INT. NURSERY - MORNING

Nana picks the Rabbit up off the counterpane and holds him at arm's length, turning him to the window light. Something in the worn face stops her.

NANA
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

EXT. WOOD - EVENING

A long June evening among the tree trunks. The Boy sets the Rabbit down in a little nest of bracken, pats it comfortable, then wanders off to pick flowers and play at brigands.

The Rabbit lies watching ants run to and fro between his velvet paws.

The tall bracken parts. Two wild rabbits creep out - THISTLE and BRAMBLE - furry and brand-new, no seams showing, changing shape queerly as they move, long and thin one minute, fat and bunchy the next. Their noses twitch.

The Rabbit stares hard to see which side the clockwork sticks out. There is none.

THISTLE
Why don't you get up and play with us?

THE RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

THISTLE
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

Thistle gives a big hop sideways and stands up on his hind legs.

THISTLE
I don't believe you can!

THE RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

THISTLE
Can you hop on your hind legs?

A dreadful question. The back of the Rabbit is made all in one piece, like a pincushion. He sits very still in the bracken.

THE RABBIT
I don't want to!

Thistle stretches out his neck and looks.

THISTLE
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

THE RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

THISTLE
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

Thistle whirls round and dances until the Rabbit is quite dizzy.

THE RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But all the while he was longing to dance, for a funny new tickly feeling ran through him.

Thistle stops dancing and comes so close that his long whiskers brush the Velveteen Rabbit's ear. He wrinkles his nose suddenly, flattens his ears, and jumps backwards.

THISTLE
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

THE RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps. The Boy runs past near them - a stamp of feet, a flash of white tails, and the strangers are gone.

THE RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

No answer. Only the little ants running to and fro, and the bracken swaying gently where the two strangers passed.

The sun sinks lower. White moths flutter out. The Boy comes and gathers him up and carries him home.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Autumn rain on the glass. The Rabbit on the pillow: whiskers loved clean off, the pink lining of his ears gone grey, his brown spots faded, his shape almost lost.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Weeks passed, and the little Rabbit grew very old and shabby, but the Boy loved him just as much. To him he was always beautiful, and that was all that the little Rabbit cared about.

The Boy's face is flushed, very red. He talks in his sleep. His little body is so hot that it burns the Rabbit held against it.

A lamp burns all night. Strange people come and go: DOCTOR HALLAM, black coat and bag, bends over the bed; Nana carries basins; a sheet is hung across the doorway.

The Rabbit slides down out of sight under the bedclothes and does not stir.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He never stirred, for he was afraid that if they found him some one might take him away, and he knew that the Boy needed him.

The house quiets. The Rabbit creeps up close to the pillow.

THE RABBIT
(whispering)
When you are well we shall go out in the garden among the flowers and the butterflies, and play splendid games in the raspberry thicket, like we used to.

The Boy's breathing eases. Sweat cools on his forehead.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And presently the fever turned, and the Boy got better.

